th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/folk-theorem.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/folk-theorem.html","questions":[{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_folk_theorem_5aff5236_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The course folk theorem is proved with strategies that, after any deviation, revert forever to a stage-game Nash equilibrium $a^*$. Why is carrying out this punishment credible, so that the strategies are subgame perfect?","options":["Playing $a^*$ forever is a Nash equilibrium of every such subgame.","The punishment gives the deviator her lowest payoff in the stage game.","With $\\delta$ close to $1$, every threat in a repeated game is credible.","Reverting to $a^*$ is Pareto efficient, so no player wants to stop it."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ce91b7e80206c8a57fdc53c5","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_folk_theorem_5aff5236_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"In the infinitely repeated prisoner's dilemma ($v(C,C)=(3,3)$, $v(C,D)=(0,5)$, $v(D,C)=(5,0)$, $v(D,D)=(1,1)$, actions observed, discount factor $\\delta\\in(0,1)$), both players use grim trigger with target $(C,C)$ and punishment $(D,D)$. For which $\\delta$ is this an SPNE with average payoffs $(3,3)$?","options":["$\\delta\\ge\\tfrac{2}{5}$","$\\delta\\ge\\tfrac{2}{3}$","$\\delta\\ge\\tfrac{1}{2}$","Every $\\delta\\in(0,1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2339f9f94b80c008878a6bec","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_folk_theorem_5aff5236_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The course folk theorem assumes that past actions are observed. Why is this assumption needed for the grim-trigger strategies used in its proof?","options":["Observed actions make the stage game have a unique Nash equilibrium that can serve as punishment.","Punishment is triggered by a deviation, so a deviation must be detected to deter it.","Without observation the feasible set would shrink to the stage Nash payoffs, whatever $\\delta$ is.","Observation guarantees that players are patient enough, since they see the long-run gains."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"131c4ef6b28b4d139f19c264","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"}]}}